A||||15||liebowa||Adding a wing to a "wingless" 87 GT
Z||000000||liebowa||04-17-99||04:54 AM||||I just purchased an 87 Fiero GT that has no spoiler, but I'd like to add one.  The color of the car is gold.<p>Anybody have any experiencee in doing this?
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||04-17-99||05:22 AM||||It's actually *very* simple to add the wing. All you need to do is drill 4 holes in the decklid. Check the bottom of the decklid to see if you can spot any filled holes (a lot of times there were holes already for the wing, but were filled up) and use those.<p>I've done it once and it was a 15 minute job.
Z||000002||theogre||04-17-99||03:17 PM||||And you've gotten hit in the head by it how many times Cliff?<p>You should ether replace the desk lid springs, or add a gas tube. Rodney Dickman has gas tube kits that basically bolt in, and look nice. I can't find a link right now. niether is hard, but will save you getting whacked in the bean if a breaze catches it.
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||04-17-99||04:53 PM||||The original springs are made so the decklid would open gently (and stay open) once a wing has been installed. AFAIK, <b>all</b> factory springs where made with the wing in mind. I've seen people install wings dozens of times an never did they need to replace the springs... Neither did mine.
Z||000004||theogre||04-18-99||03:38 PM||||actually there are 3 diferant sets of deck springs. one for plain, one for rack, one for wing. If you've been lucky enough not to have a problem great. But then some lucky people aren't in the trunk as much as I am.... got to carry my computer tools and laptop some place.<p>If there is a problem, dickmans addons are probably the nicest fix. handling those rods can be a real ________________!
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||04-18-99||03:55 PM||||Hmmm, I wasn't aware they really used three different springs. Easiest way to figure out what kind of springs are on your Fiero: press the lid release button, and see if it pops up completely. If it does, you can safely install a wing (it shouldn't pop up completely by itself). If it doesn't, then you need other springs I guess...
Z||000006||theogre||04-18-99||09:38 PM||||hey,,, if it stays up with the wing...... leave it be. I can't be right ALL the time :):) <p>Some of it depends on the specific wing you add. some of those after market ones may be heavier than stock ones.<p>If you have a pre 86 car w/o the high mount brake light, adding a wing off something with the light in the wing is a good way to modernize the lighting. (you'll need to find a control module from a "cyclops" or other after market high mount kit to make it work right.)
Z||000007||PaUL||04-19-99||02:33 PM||||I also read somewhere that Fieros came with 3 different springs. My decklid doesnt stay up with the wing on but does with it off.
Z||000008||Bozone||04-21-99||04:07 PM||||Would it be possible to fill the wing with helium? That would make the deck much lighter.
Z||000009||Rob||04-21-99||04:17 PM||||Down on the spring hook you have three settings that are changed with the spring pin, to add more tension move the pin to the back on notch. 
Z||000010||Patrick||04-21-99||04:25 PM||||<br>What Bozone is suggesting is similar to what I do when I need to work under the car. I deflate the tires, and then I fill them up with helium. With just the right amount, I can raise the car about three feet off the ground. It's important to empty the tires of all helium before driving again, otherwise the car just sort of wants to float down the highway.<p>
Z||000011||Pretender||04-21-99||04:35 PM||||I agree, last time my car broke down i filled the tires w/ helium and tied a dog leash to the front bumper...walked it the whole way home....:o)
Z||000012||Cliff Pennock||04-21-99||05:27 PM||||Last time I filled my car with Helium the engine kinda sounded like Donald Duck...
Z||000013||RFMike||05-10-99||05:50 AM||||You're killing me.
Z||000014||Bozone||05-10-99||10:13 PM||||I just got back from the great Fiero Swap Meet in Alabama and put a wing I picked up there on my GT. It was a special gas-filled wing and you have to be careful. The first time I open the deck, the helium in the wing raised the deck up with such force that the whole back of the car left the ground and the left rear tire landed on my foot. Do a few test openings first before you just "let it go" up all by itself. Whew!
Z||000015||LEE498||05-10-99||10:55 PM||||What is your deck made of steel!? It lifted the whole rear up and the tire landed on your foot!!!! a fiber glass deck! OK WHAT EVER YOU SAY*****
